            <ArticleTitle>[Headache and transient aphasia in a 35-year-old female patient].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>624-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00108-013-3253-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We describe the case of a 35-year-old female patient who suffered from fulminant tick-borne encephalitis and subsequently died. Remarkable about this case was that the woman was not living in an endemic area and that the disease occurred outside the usual season. Furthermore, this indicates that an increase in transmission of tick-borne encephalitis can be expected outside the classical endemic areas in higher altitudes, possibly as a consequence of climate changes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
